    "candidate_text": "Let \\(p=4t+3\\) and set \\(m=(p-1)/2=2t+1\\), which is odd.\n\nSuppose first that \\(a\\) is a primitive root modulo \\(p\\). Then \\(\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=p-1=2m\\). Since \\(a^{2m}\\equiv1\\pmod p\\) and \\(a^m\\not\\equiv1\\pmod p\\), while \\((a^m)^2\\equiv1\\pmod p\\), we have \\(a^m\\equiv-1\\pmod p\\). Hence\n\\[\n(-a)^m=(-1)^m a^m\\equiv(-1)(-1)\\equiv1\\pmod p.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)\\mid m\\). Conversely, if \\((-a)^n\\equiv1\\pmod p\\), then \\(a^{2n}=(-a)^{2n}\\equiv1\\pmod p\\). Since \\(\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=2m\\), this implies \\(2m\\mid2n\\), hence \\(m\\mid n\\). Therefore \\(\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m=(p-1)/2\\).\n\nConversely, suppose \\(\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m\\). Then\n\\[\na^m=(-1)^m(-a)^m\\equiv-1\\pmod p,\n\\]\nbecause \\(m\\) is odd. Also \\(a^{2m}\\equiv1\\pmod p\\). If \\(a^n\\equiv1\\pmod p\\), then \\((-a)^{2n}=a^{2n}\\equiv1\\pmod p\\), so \\(m\\mid2n\\). Since \\(m\\) is odd, \\(m\\mid n\\); write \\(n=mq\\). Now\n\\[\n1\\equiv a^n=(a^m)^q\\equiv(-1)^q\\pmod p.\n\\]\nAs \\(p\\) is odd, this forces \\(q\\) to be even. Hence \\(2m\\mid n\\). Since \\(a^{2m}\\equiv1\\pmod p\\), it follows that \\(\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=2m=p-1\\). Thus \\(a\\) is a primitive root modulo \\(p\\).",
